  "prompt_text": "The figure gives an overview of how several advanced sensing methods could be used to monitor underground and surface gas storage. It combines a cutaway view of the subsurface with instruments on the ground, showing different measurement approaches aimed at detecting stored gas and leaks.\n\nDeep gas storage is shown in a large underground reservoir accessed by a borehole, while a shallower storage body sits closer to the surface. A borehole instrument labeled as a magneto-optical trap appears to probe the deep reservoir, and red wave-like arcs suggest acoustic or signal responses from the stored gas. Along the left side, quantum-enhanced distributed-acoustic sensing uses a cable-like line extending downward to detect subsurface activity. Above ground, atomic gravimeters measure changes over the shallow storage area, implying detection through gravity variations caused by the gas underground.\n\nThe surface facilities include tanks and equipment, with a visible leaking gas plume. Two remote optical methods monitor this leak from a distance: a dual comb spectrometer mounted on a tower and a single-photon LiDAR unit. Overall, the image highlights a multi-sensor monitoring strategy that combines underground, surface, and remote measurements to track gas storage integrity and detect emissions.",
